! Problem 2.2: Length of an Unknown File, Method 2 "End byte in $R,
! progressive indexing" (C22-6578 Programming Examples, p.12).
!
! Same problem as problem_2_2_method1.strap: information of unknown
! length is written in consecutive 7-bit bytes beginning at INFO,
! terminated by the first all-ones (2^7-1 = 127) byte; find the file
! length (including the terminator) in bits, in the value field of $1.
! Reuses Method 1's own test data (bytes 5, 12, 100, 127) so the two
! methods' results are directly comparable: expected length = 4*7 =
! 28 bits either way.
!
! The six instruction lines (LV through BEW) and VFIELD's VF are
! copied verbatim from the manual; INFO is supplied the same way
! Method 1's INFO was.
!
! How this differs from Method 1, and why it needs a fix this session
! found (see strap/README.md "Gotchas"): $1 is loaded once via LV,
! then advanced entirely by PROGRESSIVE indexing inside the compare
! loop itself -- COMP's "(V+I)" adds its own address part (0.07 = 7
! bits, the byte width) to $1's VALUE every iteration, using $1's
! *current* VALUE (not COMP's own address field) as the actual memory
! address being compared (StretchExecute.bsv's VflInt case: effective
! address = xIdx.addr in progressive mode, the instruction's own A24
! is the increment amount, not part of the address). So after N
! comparisons $1's VALUE is INFO's bit address plus 7*N -- an absolute
! address, not a length. The final "L(BU,7)(V-I), INFO($1)" instruction
! is ALSO progressive: its effective address is (again) $1's *current*
! value, and its own address part -- the literal INFO -- is what gets
! *subtracted* from $1 afterward. That's comment (a)'s "serves mainly
! to perform the (V-I) operation": it isn't really loading INFO($1)
! for the load's own sake (the loaded value is discarded), it's using
! progressive indexing's address-part-as-increment mechanic to
! subtract INFO's own bit address back off $1, turning "INFO's address
! + 7N" into exactly "7N" -- the file length in bits, landing in $1's
! VALUE field as the problem asks.
!
! This is also the first program in this project to use VFL Immediate
! addressing (P=4): "LI(BU,7), 127" loads the literal 127 into the
! accumulator's rightmost 7 bits, not from a memory address -- LI
! isn't its own mnemonic (Appendix A doesn't list one): it's "L" with
! the "I" (Immediate) suffix from Appendix A footnote 4. Getting a
! bare integer literal like 127 to mean "the immediate value 127" (as
! opposed to a bit address 127) needed two real fixes to strap_asm.py:
! (1) the "I"-suffix strip only worked when no dds was given -- see
! strap/README.md "Gotchas". (2) more subtly, C22-6578's own comment
! on this exact instruction (p.13, following Method 2) confirms the
! *encoding*: a plain (non-"point") integer used where an address is
! normally expected is an "integer address" that STRAP must convert
! to a bit address using the operation's *implied subfield* -- for
! this instruction, 7 bits (the field length) rather than a VFL
! address's usual 24 -- giving bit address 0.(127*2^-7*2^24) =
! 260096.0. That is exactly encode_vfl_immediate's (24-len)-bit
! left-shift, packing 127 as 0xFE0000 in the 24-bit A24 field rather
! than literal 127 -- verified by hand against the manual's own worked
! arithmetic before this file was written.

START   LV, $1, VFIELD
        LI(BU,7), 127          !or (2)1111111
COMP    K(BU, 7) (V+I), 0.07($1)
        BAE, $+1.0
        B, COMP
        L(BU, 7) (V-I), INFO($1)
        BEW, $
VFIELD  VF, INFO
INFO    DD(BU,7,8), 5,12,100,127

        END, START
